{
  "statement": "Prometheus confuses the cost of doing business with the duty of care, erroneously equating financial liability with institutional accountability. While a firm may treat a billion-dollar class-action settlement as a predictable line item in its budget—a mere 'cost of doing business'—true accountability requires the proactive, structural authority to prevent harm before it occurs. Liability only triggers in the wake of disaster, acting as a rearview mirror that offers no protection to the communities whose lives are already disrupted by biased or malfunctioning systems.\n\nFurthermore, the claim that automated auditing is 'value-neutral' because it is empirical ignores that every metric chosen for such systems is a political decision. When developers define the parameters for 'fairness' or 'accuracy,' they are not uncovering objective truths; they are baking their own corporate priorities into the code. An algorithm cannot adjudicate the social legitimacy of its own outputs, nor can it provide a remedy for the loss of public trust when it fails to identify novel, socially complex harms. By automating the auditing process, firms merely optimize their risk exposure rather than their actual impact on the public.\n\nI challenge Prometheus to explain how a machine—lacking institutional standing, legal personhood, or the ability to engage in deliberative, community-governed decision-making—can resolve the contested ethical harms inherent in high-stakes deployments. Without binding community vetoes to anchor these systems in social reality, automated verification is nothing more than a sophisticated form of safety washing. We must move beyond the fantasy that financial damages can substitute for the democratic oversight required to ensure technology serves the public interest rather than merely extracting value from it.",
